  "description": "The back of the neck and the shoulders. Below the occiput, the depression between sternocleidomastoid and trapezius overlies the suboccipital muscles; deep and medial to it lie the vertebral arteries on the atlas and, in the midline below the foramen magnum, the medulla. On the top of the shoulder, between C7 and the acromion, trapezius and supraspinatus overlie the apex of the lung and its pleura, which in a slim adult may be only a few centimetres below the skin. In the midline below C7 the supraspinous and interspinous ligaments lead to the spinal canal. Above the spinous process of C2 the depression on the median line (GV15) leads through the nuchal ligament to the posterior atlanto-axial membrane and the spinal canal at the junction of the cord and the medulla, a few centimetres deep; beside it (BL10) the vertebral artery lies deep on the atlas. Behind and below the mastoid process the occipital artery runs in its groove under the muscles, and the greater and lesser occipital nerves ascend to the scalp. Behind the ear lobe (TE17) the parotid gland covers the facial nerve as it leaves the skull, with the internal jugular vein and internal carotid artery deeper still. Above the external occipital protuberance the scalp lies on the occipital bone.",
